  "summary": "Understand and use six well-known Arabic proverbs, explain what a proverb means, and use مَنْ (whoever) in sayings like مَنْ جَدَّ وَجَدَ.",
  "intro": "Proverbs, أَمْثَال, are everywhere in Arabic: in conversation, in speeches and on the walls of shops. Many of them use مَنْ, which here does not mean 'who?' but 'whoever': مَنْ جَدَّ وَجَدَ (whoever works hard succeeds). The verbs are in the past, but the meaning is always true. Others are short sentences without a verb: الصَّبْرُ مِفْتَاحُ الْفَرَجِ (patience is the key to relief). To explain a proverb, say يَعْنِي (it means).",
  "objectives": [
    "Understand six common Arabic proverbs and say when people use them",
    "Use مَنْ + a past verb for a general truth (whoever…)",
    "Explain a proverb in simple Arabic with يَعْنِي",
    "Use the words for patience, wisdom, mind and neighbour"
  ],
